>> title: Xíu Mại
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: pork, tomato, vietnamese, meatballs, stovetop
>> counters: Phở & Bánh Mì, One Pot
>> aka: xiu mai, xíu mại, Vietnamese meatballs, pork meatballs in tomato sauce, banh mi xiu mai, meatball banh mi, Vietnamese meatball sandwich, S4
>> pairs-with: banh-mi-khong
>> servings: 4
>> time: 45 min
>> slack: unforgiving — pork balls poached in the sauce reach temperature or they do not, and there is no telling from outside; the twenty minutes of poaching is the safety and the four minutes frying the paste out at the start is the flavour, and neither substitutes for the other
>> step.1: mix, throw, roll 12 balls
>> step.2: fry 4 min, cook the paste out
>> step.3: simmer 10 min, thin and sweet
>> step.4: poach 20 min, spooning over
>> step.5: scatter and serve wet

Mix @ground pork{1%lb}(450 g), @grated jicama{1/3%cup}(45 g; squeezed dry), @shallot{1}(minced), @garlic{2%cloves}(minced), @fish sauce{1%Tbs}(15 mL), @granulated sugar{1%tsp} and @cornstarch{1%Tbs}(8 g), throw the mixture against the side of the bowl a dozen times so it tightens, then roll it into 12 balls.

Fry @yellow onion{1/2}(diced small) in @annatto oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL) in a #skillet{} ~fry{4%min} until soft and orange, then stir @tomato paste{2%Tbs}(35 g) through and cook it a minute past raw.

Simmer @&(~1)tomato base{} with @grated tomatoes{2%large}(450 g; grated on the box grater, skins discarded) and @chicken stock{1 1/2%cup}(355 mL) and @fish sauce{1%Tbs}(15 mL) ~simmer{10%min}. The sauce should stay thin enough to run off a spoon. Thickening it is the usual mistake — a thick sauce sits on top of the bread instead of soaking into it, and soaking in is the entire point.

Poach @&(~3)meatballs{} in @&(~1)sauce{} ~poach{20%min}, turning them once and spooning sauce over as they firm up.

Scatter @scallions{2}(sliced) and @cilantro{2%Tbs} over @&(~1)xíu mại{} and bring it to the table in a shallow bowl with @bánh mì rolls{2} to tear and dip — or split a roll open and spoon the balls straight in, wet enough to run down your wrist.
